  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "Figure 1, ‘The health labour market framework’. At the top, a wide bar ‘Economy, population and broader societal drivers’ sends downward arrows into two main domains: ‘Education sector’ (left, orange) and ‘Labour market dynamics’ (right, pink/blue). In the Education sector, ‘High school’ (vertical box) branches to ‘Education in health’ and ‘Education in other fields’; ‘Education in health’ leads to ‘Pool of qualified health workers’ inside Labour market dynamics, which connects bidirectionally via ‘Migration’ with ‘Abroad’ below and branches rightward to ‘Employed’, ‘Unemployed’, and ‘Out of labour force’. ‘Employed’ feeds into ‘Health care sector’ (and ‘Other sectors’ below, with bidirectional flow between them), which leads to ‘Health workforce equipped to deliver quality health service’ and finally to the vertical outcome bar ‘Universal health coverage with safe, effective, person-centred health services’ on the far right. Beneath the flow, three policy boxes point upward to their respective stages — ‘Policies on production’ (infrastructure and material, enrolment, selecting students, teaching staff), ‘Policies to address inflows and outflows’ (migration and emigration, attracting unemployed health workers, bringing health workers back into the health care sector), and ‘Policies to address maldistribution and inefficiencies’ (productivity and performance, skill mix composition, retaining health workers in underserved areas) — above a full-width bottom bar, ‘Policies to regulate the private sector’ (manage dual practice, improve quality of training, enhance service delivery).",
